//BUG="the right-hand side of a destructuring assignment is lowered twice"
//OUT="hi 5"

// Not in FEEDBACK.md. `lower_statement` (src/compiler/lowerer/mod.rs) lowers
// both sides of an assignment, and only then notices that the left-hand side
// is a struct/tuple pattern. It hands the *already lowered* value to
// `gen_struct_destructuring` and lowers the statement it builds a second time.
//
// Lowering is not idempotent: a string literal becomes
// `_Str_from_raw(<string>)`, and the inner node is still a raw string, so the
// second pass wraps it again into `_Str_from_raw(_Str_from_raw(<string>))`.
// `_Str_from_raw` takes a `_string`, not a `Str`, so the call has no
// candidate and the program is rejected.
//
// `a, b = x, 5` is fine because a variable survives being lowered twice; only
// a literal string on the right-hand side of a destructuring assignment
// trips it. `P x a y b = P x "hi" y 5` fails the same way.
//
// The fix is to destructure before lowering the value, or to pass the
// unlowered value into `gen_struct_destructuring`.

fn main
    a, b = "hi", 5
    out "{a} {b}"
